---
// Standalone from the rest of the site on purpose: this is a 2021 university
// project kept at its original URL, with its own Space Mono look rather than
// the site theme.
import '../../styles/choropleths.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title>Revere Choropleths</title>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Interactive choropleth maps of City of Revere public works data and census demographics, by census tract."
    />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Mono:wght@400;700&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="main">
      <h1>Revere Choropleth Maps</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Interactive maps of city services and demographics in Revere,
        Massachusetts, aggregated to US Census tracts.
      </p>

      <ul class="cards">
        <li>
          <a class="button" href="./dpw-data/">DPW Data</a>
          <span>
            183 categories of Department of Public Works jobs completed &mdash;
            potholes, street cleaning, drains, snow, parks, water, signs and
            more.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="button" href="./race-data/">Race and Ethnicity Data</a>
          <span>
            Population by race and ethnicity from the 2014&ndash;2019 5-year
            American Community Survey.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to read these</h2>
      <p>
        Each map shades every census tract in Revere by a count: for the DPW
        maps that is the number of jobs of the selected type completed in that
        tract, and for the demographic maps it is the number of residents in
        that group. Darker means more. The buttons sit below each map: on the
        DPW page, pick a department and then a job type; on the demographics
        page there is a single list. Hover a tract to see its exact value.
      </p>
      <p class="caveat">
        Counts are raw, not per-capita &mdash; a tract with more people or more
        roads will naturally show more jobs. Read them as &ldquo;where the work
        happened&rdquo;, not as a rate.
      </p>

      <h2>Where this came from</h2>
      <p>
        These were built for the
        <strong>City of Revere Service Data Project</strong>
        (BU CS506 Data Science / BU Spark!, Spring 2021), a study of whether city
        services in Revere are distributed equitably across demographic lines. The
        client was the City of Revere; the team was Yuanming Chai, Mackenzie Knox,
        Quinn Relyea, Giancarlo Sirio and myself, with Lingyang Jiang as project manager.
      </p>
      <p>
        I owned the DPW pipeline behind these maps: cleaning the city&rsquo;s
        raw work-order export, geocoding addresses to coordinates, joining each
        job to its census tract with Census TIGER shapefiles, and generating the
        maps you see here. The DPW headline finding was that
        <strong>Tract 1708 receives the most services of any tract</strong>, in
        nearly every category.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full report&rsquo;s statistical tests found no significant city-wide
        disparity in 311 reporting by race, largely because ACS data can&rsquo;t
        be segmented finely enough. The maps linked above are the exploratory
        layer underneath that conclusion.
      </p>

      <p class="links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/BU-Spark/CS506Spring2021Repository/tree/master/City%20of%20Revere%20Service%20Data%20Project"
        >
          Source and notebooks on GitHub
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
